Task 1: Set up Camel K on Kubernetes
Subtask 1.1: Verify Kubernetes Cluster Status
First, let's ensure your Kubernetes cluster is running properly.

Open a terminal in your cloud machine
Check the cluster status:
kubectl cluster-info
Verify nodes are ready:
kubectl get nodes
Check if all system pods are running:
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
Expected output should show all pods in Running status.

Subtask 1.2: Install Camel K Operator
The Camel K operator manages the lifecycle of Camel integrations in Kubernetes.

Download and install the Camel K CLI:
# Download the latest Camel K CLI
curl -Lo kamel https://github.com/apache/camel-k/releases/download/v1.12.0/camel-k-client-1.12.0-linux-64bit.tar.gz

# Extract and make executable
tar -xzf camel-k-client-1.12.0-linux-64bit.tar.gz
sudo mv kamel /usr/local/bin/
chmod +x /usr/local/bin/kamel
Verify the installation:
kamel version
Install the Camel K operator in your cluster:
kamel install
Wait for the operator to be ready:
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod -l name=camel-k-operator -n camel-k --timeout=300s
Subtask 1.3: Verify Camel K Installation
Check the Camel K operator status:
kubectl get pods -n camel-k
Verify the integration platform:
kamel get platform
The platform should show as Ready.

Task 2: Deploy a Camel Route as a Serverless Function
Subtask 2.1: Create a Simple REST API Integration
Let's create a simple Camel route that exposes a REST API endpoint.

Create a new directory for your integration files:
mkdir ~/camel-k-lab
cd ~/camel-k-lab
Create your first Camel K integration file:
cat > hello-api.java << 'EOF'
// camel-k: language=java

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class HelloApi extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        
        // REST configuration
        restConfiguration()
            .component("platform-http")
            .port(8080);
        
        // REST endpoint definition
        rest("/api")
            .get("/hello")
                .produces("application/json")
                .to("direct:hello")
            .get("/hello/{name}")
                .produces("application/json")
                .to("direct:hello-name");
        
        // Route for simple hello
        from("direct:hello")
            .setBody(constant("{\"message\": \"Hello from Camel K!\", \"timestamp\": \"${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}\"}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
        
        // Route for personalized hello
        from("direct:hello-name")
            .setBody(simple("{\"message\": \"Hello ${header.name} from Camel K!\", \"timestamp\": \"${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}\"}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
    }
}
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Deploy the Integration
Deploy the integration using Camel K:
kamel run hello-api.java --name hello-api
Monitor the deployment progress:
kamel logs hello-api
Check the integration status:
kamel get
Wait until the integration shows Running status.

Subtask 2.3: Test the Serverless Function
Get the service information:
kubectl get services
Port-forward to access the service locally:
kubectl port-forward service/hello-api 8080:80 &
Test the REST endpoints:
# Test simple hello endpoint
curl http://localhost:8080/api/hello

# Test personalized hello endpoint
curl http://localhost:8080/api/hello/Student
Expected responses:

{"message": "Hello from Camel K!", "timestamp": "2024-01-15 10:30:45"}
{"message": "Hello Student from Camel K!", "timestamp": "2024-01-15 10:30:50"}
Subtask 2.4: Create a Data Processing Integration
Now let's create a more complex integration that processes data.

Create a file processing integration:
cat > file-processor.java << 'EOF'
// camel-k: language=java

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class FileProcessor extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        
        // File processing route
        from("timer:trigger?period=30000")
            .setBody(constant("Processing batch at ${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}"))
            .log("${body}")
            .to("direct:process-data");
        
        from("direct:process-data")
            .transform(simple("Processed: ${body}"))
            .log("Data processed: ${body}")
            .to("log:file-processor?level=INFO");
        
        // Health check endpoint
        from("platform-http:/health")
            .setBody(constant("{\"status\": \"UP\", \"service\": \"file-processor\"}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
    }
}
EOF
Deploy the file processor:
kamel run file-processor.java --name file-processor
Monitor the logs to see the processing:
kamel logs file-processor
Task 3: Monitor and manage the Camel K deployment using Kubernetes tools
Subtask 3.1: Monitor Integration Status
View all running integrations:
kamel get
Get detailed information about a specific integration:
kamel describe hello-api
Check the underlying Kubernetes resources:
kubectl get pods -l camel.apache.org/integration=hello-api
kubectl get services -l camel.apache.org/integration=hello-api
Subtask 3.2: Scale and Manage Integrations
Scale the hello-api integration:
kubectl scale deployment hello-api --replicas=3
Verify the scaling:
kubectl get pods -l camel.apache.org/integration=hello-api
Check resource usage:
kubectl top pods -l camel.apache.org/integration=hello-api
Subtask 3.3: Configure Integration Properties
Create a configuration file for your integration:
cat > application.properties << 'EOF'
# Application configuration
greeting.message=Hello from Configured Camel K
server.port=8080
logging.level.org.apache.camel=INFO
EOF
Create a ConfigMap from the properties file:
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=application.properties
Create an enhanced integration that uses the configuration:
cat > configured-api.java << 'EOF'
// camel-k: language=java property=configmap:app-config

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class ConfiguredApi extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        
        restConfiguration()
            .component("platform-http")
            .port("{{server.port}}");
        
        rest("/api/v2")
            .get("/greeting")
                .produces("application/json")
                .to("direct:configured-greeting");
        
        from("direct:configured-greeting")
            .setBody(simple("{\"message\": \"{{greeting.message}}\", \"version\": \"2.0\", \"timestamp\": \"${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}\"}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"))
            .log("Served configured greeting: ${body}");
    }
}
EOF
Deploy the configured integration:
kamel run configured-api.java --name configured-api --property configmap:app-config
Subtask 3.4: Monitor with Kubernetes Native Tools
Create a monitoring script to watch your integrations:
cat > monitor-integrations.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Camel K Integration Status ==="
kamel get

echo -e "\n=== Pod Status ==="
kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/component=integration

echo -e "\n=== Service Status ==="
kubectl get services -l camel.apache.org/integration

echo -e "\n=== Resource Usage ==="
kubectl top pods -l app.kubernetes.io/component=integration 2>/dev/null || echo "Metrics server not available"

echo -e "\n=== Recent Events ==="
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail -10
EOF

chmod +x monitor-integrations.sh
Run the monitoring script:
./monitor-integrations.sh
Set up continuous monitoring:
watch -n 10 './monitor-integrations.sh'
Press Ctrl+C to stop the monitoring.

Subtask 3.5: Implement Health Checks and Observability
Create an integration with comprehensive health checks:
cat > health-integration.java << 'EOF'
// camel-k: language=java

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class HealthIntegration extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        
        // Health check endpoint
        from("platform-http:/health")
            .setBody(constant("{\"status\": \"UP\", \"checks\": {\"database\": \"UP\", \"external-service\": \"UP\"}}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
        
        // Metrics endpoint
        from("platform-http:/metrics")
            .setBody(simple("{\"requests\": ${exchangeProperty.CamelMessageHistory.size()}, \"uptime\": \"${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}\"}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
        
        // Ready endpoint for Kubernetes readiness probe
        from("platform-http:/ready")
            .setBody(constant("{\"ready\": true}"))
            .setHeader("Content-Type", constant("application/json"));
        
        // Main business logic
        from("timer:business?period=60000")
            .log("Business process executed at ${date:now}")
            .to("log:business-metrics?level=INFO");
    }
}
EOF
Deploy with health checks:
kamel run health-integration.java --name health-integration
Test the health endpoints:
# Port forward the service
kubectl port-forward service/health-integration 8081:80 &

# Test health endpoints
curl http://localhost:8081/health
curl http://localhost:8081/metrics
curl http://localhost:8081/ready
